ction. For a small expression or head turn, keep her head and shoulders comfortably inside the frame. For walking or a larger body gesture, widen to a three-quarter or full-body view so the movement remains readable. Match the reference person's facial proportions, eye shape, nose, lips, hairline, hairstyle, outfit, and visible accessories. Preserve the reference's lighting direction and overall color relationships. Keep the background recognizable through the shot, with natural perspective and a depth of field suited to a portrait. Let existing background objects remain stationary as the subject moves.\n\nThe requested action and camera direction for this shot are: {b}\n\nDevelop the gesture smoothly over the full available clip. The opening establishes her starting pose with calm, settled body weight. The middle carries the requested action through a physically continuous movement. The final portion lets the gesture resolve into a relaxed pose rather than an abrupt freeze. Use small intermediate changes in her head angle, gaze, cheeks, and shoulders so the action reads as a continuous performance. Keep the eyes aligned with the head movement and let the neck and shoulders follow naturally. Maintain coherent hair strands and fabric folds as they respond to the motion.\n\nFavor natural skin texture, soft tonal transitions, and stable facial features across frames. Maintain ordinary facial asymmetry and consistent complexion. Render eyelashes, lips, jewelry, and clothing edges with coherent detail at the selected resolution. Hands, when they are visible in the reference framing, remain anatomically consistent and move only when the requested gesture needs them. Keep the outfit and accessories consistent throughout the shot.\n\nExecute the requested camera move at a steady pace. Favor low amplitude when the action description does not request a larger movement. Preserve a consistent lens perspective and stable exposure, with smooth focus behavior centered on the face.